Q: Splitting a string into tokens with several possible separators, using `std::ranges` A: <p>Out of curiosity, here is another proposal based on <code>std::views::split</code> that can be quite fast (based on C++23 though).</p>
<p>Actually, the annoying part is to deal with several separators (mainly for performance), so the idea here is simply to use <code>split</code> with only one separator <strong>after having replaced all the separators by a single one</strong> (say the first one for instance). This is the easy part; here <code>delims</code> is a variadic parameters pack holding the different delimiters:</p>
<pre><code>std::array delimiters = {delims...};

auto tokens = str
    | std::views::transform([&amp;](auto&amp;&amp; c) {
        return ((c==delims) || ...) ? delimiters[0] : c; });
}
</code></pre>
<p>Unfortunately, we can't any more build a <code>std::string_view</code> from here, so we have to deal with indexes instead and associate each character with its index in the sequence, which implies to use <code>std::views::enumerate</code> (and therefore C++23). Another issue is that <code>std::views::split</code> can't take as parameter such a couple <code>idx,character</code> as a delimiter (and there is no projection feature here), so we have to create some <code>duo</code> struct that holds this information, with an overload of <code>operator==</code> to make <code>std::views::split</code> happy</p>
<pre><code>struct duo {
    std::size_t i={};
    char c={};
    bool operator==(duo other) const { return c==other.c; }
};
</code></pre>
<p>We can then finish the definition of the <code>tokens</code> view:</p>
<pre><code>    auto tokens = str
        | std::views::transform([&amp;](auto&amp;&amp; c) {
            return ((c==delims) || ...) ? delimiters[0] : c; })
        | std::views::enumerate
        | std::views::transform([](auto t)  { return std::make_from_tuple&lt;duo&gt;(t); })
        | std::views::split (duo{0,delimiters[0]})
        | std::views::filter([](auto&amp;&amp; rng) { return !rng.empty(); })
        | std::views::transform([&amp;](auto&amp;&amp; rng) {
            return std::string_view(
                str.begin()+ rng[0].i,
                str.begin()+ rng[0].i + rng.size()
            );  })
        ;
</code></pre>
<p>We can use it as follows with 4 different delimiters for instance</p>
<pre><code>int main() {
    std::string line{&quot;\tSplit   \t\n\t   this sequence\t of  ; tokens  &quot;};
    auto tokens = tokenize_with_ranges&lt;' ','\t','\n',';'&gt;(line);
    for (const auto&amp; token : tokens) {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; token &lt;&lt; '\n';
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/dPEY9eeKh" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>Now, here is the <a href="https://quick-bench.com/q/C8HPA89l44haPrJkvH5F02aRsXY" rel="nofollow noreferrer">benchmark part</a>, where a <code>BM_TokenizeWithRangesGeneric</code> entry for this templated <code>tokenize_with_ranges</code> has been added (with 4 delimiters in the example).</p>
<p>It is almost as fast as the <code>BM_TokenizeWithFind</code> version, and it should not (almost) depend on the number of delimiters.</p>

Q: How to best simulate pyvisa instruments? A: <p>I believe the best way to simulate pyvisa instruments is with <code>pyvisa-sim</code> and using custom <code>.yaml</code> files.</p>
<p>I referenced the following docs:<br />
<a href="https://pyvisa.readthedocs.io/projects/pyvisa-sim/en/latest/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://pyvisa.readthedocs.io/projects/pyvisa-sim/en/latest/index.html</a></p>
<p>where the secret sauce is:</p>
<pre><code>rm = pyvisa.ResourceManager('your_mock_here.yaml@sim')
</code></pre>
<p>You can pass the absolute file path to the resource manager followed by <code>@sim</code> and you will use your own custom yaml file for simulation.</p>
<p>The best example .yaml can be found in the source code here:<br />
<a href="https://github.com/pyvisa/pyvisa-sim/blob/main/pyvisa_sim/default.yaml" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/pyvisa/pyvisa-sim/blob/main/pyvisa_sim/default.yaml</a></p>
<p>and my initial custom yaml file came out like this:</p>
<pre><code>spec: &quot;1.0&quot;

# Device definitions
devices:
  generic:

    eom:
      ASRL INSTR:
        q: &quot;\r\n&quot;
        r: &quot;\n&quot;


    dialogues:
      # Query commands (returning data)
      - q: &quot;*IDN?&quot;
        r: &quot;SIM_COMP,ASRL_SIM_DEVICE,SIM0001,1.0&quot;

      - q: &quot;*OPC?&quot;
        r: &quot;1&quot;

      - q: &quot;SYST:ERR?&quot;
        r: '0,&quot;No error&quot;'

      - q: &quot;MEASUREMENT?&quot;
        r: &quot;MEAS1,PHASE&quot;

      - q: &quot;MEASUREMENT:MEAS1:RESULTS:ALLACQS:MEAN?&quot;
        r: &quot;1.2345&quot;

      - q: &quot;PING&quot;
        r: &quot;PONG&quot;

      - q: &quot;INDUCE:ERR&quot;
        r: '1,&quot;ERROR: SIM INDUCED&quot;'

      # Write-only commands (return nothing)
      - q: &quot;CONF:VOLT 1&quot;
        r: &quot;&quot;

      - q: &quot;CONF:FREQ 10&quot;
        r: &quot;&quot;

      # Catch-all WRITE (no ?)
      # Secret sauce: Ensures that no resopnse is queued 
      #   if a write command is not defined above. This is 
      #   helpful for instances where write(&quot;*SRE 0&quot;) occurs
      #   after instrument connection. 
      - q: &quot;^[^?]+$&quot;
        r: &quot;&quot;

# Resource definitions
resources:
  ASRL0::INSTR:
    device: generic
  TCPIP::localhost::INSTR:
    device: generic
</code></pre>
